<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfonso IX of León</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1171">1171</a>–<a href="/years/1230">1230</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfonso IX was king of León from 1188 to 1230. In his first year he summoned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> of León, which included townsmen alongside nobles and clergy and is often cited as the earliest European parliament of its kind. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caceres">Cáceres</a>, Mérida and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>, and his son Ferdinand III united León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon.jpg" alt="Alfonso IX of León" width="320" height="566" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adeffonsus,_king_of_Galicia_and_Leon_(detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alfonso was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a>, the only son of King Ferdinand II of León and Urraca of Portugal. His father was the younger son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII of León and Castile</a>, who divided his kingdoms between his sons, setting the stage for conflict in the family until Alfonso IX's son Ferdinand III reunited them. Alfonso had great difficulty obtaining the throne his birth entitled him to. In July 1188 his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> required him to recognise the elder as overlord in exchange for recognising his authority in León.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The difficult economic situation at the start of his reign led Alfonso to raise taxes on the poorer classes, which caused protests and a few town revolts. In response he summoned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> of León in the cloisters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-san-isidoro">Basilica of San Isidoro</a>, an assembly of nobles, clergy and city representatives, which then demanded compensatory spending and closer oversight of royal expenditure. Many historians, including John Keane, regard it as instrumental in forming democratic parliaments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The session of 1188 predates the first session of the Parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. He also founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-salamanca">University of Salamanca</a> in 1218.</p><p>His marriages brought conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>. In 1191 he married his first cousin Theresa of Portugal, and the papal legate Cardinal Gregory declared the marriage null for consanguinity. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alarcos">Battle of Alarcos</a>, Alfonso IX invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> with the aid of Muslim troops and was excommunicated by Pope Celestine III.</p><p>In 1197 he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berengaria-of-castile">Berengaria of Castile</a> to cement peace, and the kingdom was placed under interdict. Innocent III declared that marriage invalid in 1198, though they stayed together until 1204, and treaties of 1205, 1207 and 1209 forced Alfonso to concede territory. The treaty of 1207 is the first existing public document in the Castilian dialect. He captured Mérida and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a> in 1230, which allowed the later conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> under his son.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Alfonso died on 24 September 1230. His son Ferdinand III, already king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, inherited León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> as well, thanks to the negotiations of his mother Berengaria, who persuaded her stepdaughters to renounce their claim. Hearing the news, Ferdinand abandoned a campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaen">Jaén</a> and travelled to León to be crowned. The union of the two kingdoms went on to dominate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>.</p><p>Alfonso had eight children from his two marriages, both of which were annulled for consanguinity, and fathered many more with women of lower rank, among them Teresa Gil de Soverosa, with whom he had a relationship from 1218 until his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alfonso IX of León” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Crown of Castile took shape in 1230, when Ferdinand III, king of Castile since 1217, inherited the Kingdom of León on the death of his father <b>Alfonso IX</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His sister Berenguela, who had been married to <b>Alfonso IX of León</b>, passed the Castilian crown to their son Ferdinand III.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Ferdinand II and <b>Alfonso IX</b> the frontier moved south again, helped by the military orders of Santiago and Alcántara, until Alfonso IX took Cáceres in 1229 and Mérida and Badajoz in 1230.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Althing of Iceland met from about 930, <b>Alfonso IX of León</b> summoned townsmen to his Cortes in 1188, and in England Simon de Montfort’s parliament of 1265 included representatives of the towns; the word comes from the French parler, “to speak”.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Alfonso IX of León</b> took the city in 1230.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-taifa-of-badajoz">Second Taifa of Badajoz</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zamora, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Sarria, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.5,-5.76&amp;year=1209" aria-label="Zamora on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4410 684 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alfonso IX of León"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4860 954h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4860 954h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.5,-5.76&amp;year=1209">What was here in 1209? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
